  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low will pass by or through the region\nSunday into Monday.\n\nHigh pressure over the Mid-Atlantic will persist through tonight\nbefore shifting off the coast of New England on Saturday. \nTranquil conditions continue with above-normal temperatures \naround the region. \n\nMeanwhile, broad troughing continues to reside over the \nSoutheast US. As some upper energy arrives within the sub-\ntropical jet today, a closed surface low will develop and \nstrengthen to the east of Florida over the weekend.\n\nFrustratingly, guidance continues to struggle significantly on \nhow the system will evolve over the weekend, despite only be \n48-72 hours out. The latest 20/00Z guidance suite depicted by \nboth the deterministic GFS/ECMWF show two distinct areas of low \npressure developing...almost like a double barrel low set-up. \nThe weaker, broader western low will remain in close proximity\nto the Southeast coast, whereas a stronger eastern low will \ntrack northward nearing Cape Hatteras on Sunday. Not completely \nbuying these solutions, but cannot be discarded considering how \npoorly guidance has performed thus far. One thing that has \nbecome more certain though is once the low tracks up towards the\nMid-Atlantic region on Sunday, that the low is expected to be \npositioned either over our area or just off our coast by Sunday\nnight. With this being said, regardless on how the track of the\nsystem pans out before Sunday, there is increasing confidence \nin a widespread rainfall event occuring Sunday afternoon into \nearly Monday.\n\nThe National Hurricane Center has maintained has a 30 percent \nchance of Cyclone Formation within the next 48 hours, with a 50 \npercent chance within the next 7 days. Regardless of whether \nthis system acquires (sub) tropical characteristics, it is \nlikely that periods of heavy rain, gusty winds (mainly for \ncoastal areas), and coastal flooding may occur. The Weather \nPrediction Center maintains a Marginal risk for Excessive \nRainfall for the entire area excluding the Lehigh Valley and \nPocono region on Sunday into Sunday night. Early estimations on \npossible rainfall amounts are around 1 to 2 inches areawide, \nwith a localized corridor of higher totals possible, depending \non how the track of the low pans out.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another low pressure system may approach the\narea around Thanksgiving.\n\nBehind the weekend system, a strong cold front will then cross \nthrough the region on Monday. This will welcome the return of\nhigh pressure which will bring much cooler (seasonable) and\ndrier conditions to the area through mid-week. \n\nGuidance then continues to indicate a highly amplified upper\nlevel pattern where there is an increasing signal for low \npressure to develop around the middle to end of next week over\nthe Southeast US. As of this writing, the NBM has 40-70 percent\nPoPs Wednesday night into Thanksgiving Day. While it is still around\na week away, this is another disturbance worth watching as this\nmay have some implications during the holiday week.",
